A: In the ArrayList project, there is an assumption that specifies the object under test should be a non-null ArrayList object. This assumption can be validated using PexAssume.NotNull() before the method PUT_AddContract is called.
If you need to specify a null object as the object under test, simply remove the PexAssumeUnderTest attribute. The Pex infrastructure will then randomly generate a null input for the object under test. 

[PexMethod]
public void PUT_AddContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
{
   PexAssume.NotNull(arrList);
   // ...
}

I hope this helps. Enjoy testing :)
For more information and examples on using assumptions, see the documentation at: http://research.microsoft.com/en-us/um/cambridge/projects/peculiar/peculiar_book.pdf

A: You may use PexInstrument. In this case, you need to use ArrayListEx, which is a class that inherits from ArrayList, and has an additional property to be tested. 
[TestFixture, PexClass, PexInstrument]
public partial class TestArrayList
{
    [PexMethod]
    public void PUT_AddContract([PexElements(type: typeof(int))]List<int> lst, int x)
    {
        AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

        ArrayListEx arrList = new ArrayListEx(lst);

        int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
        int Old_arrListFirstIndexOfX = arrList.FirstIndexOf(x);
        int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
        bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

        int New_Ret = arrList.Add(x);

        int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
        int New_arrListFirstIndexOfX = arrList.FirstIndexOf(x);
        int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
        bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

        // Assert the results...
    }
}

ArrayListEx:
public class ArrayListEx
{
    private ArrayList list;

    public ArrayListEx(IEnumerable<int> i)
    {
        list = new ArrayList(i);
    }

    // Implement necessary methods to fit into the contract:
    // Add, Remove, Count, IndexOf, Contains, etc.

    public int Count { get { return list.Count; } }

    public int Add(int item)
    {
        return list.Add(item);
    }

    // Add more necessary methods...
}

By PexInstrumentation, Pex will automatically generate a test for each possible contract of objects. In this case, for ArrayList, Pex will create a test for each possible contract of an ArrayList, including Count, Add/Remove etc. And the key point is that you have a clear direction for what you are testing when you use PexInstrument, just like how you use PexClass.

A: You can add assumption about the input parameters to the test method. For example you can add assumptions like:
[PexMethod]
public void PUT_AddContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, [PexAssumeValid] int x)
{
    ...
}

So, Pex will test the method with different combinations of values for arrList and x. 
More information about assumptions is in the documentation.

A: To avoid using PexAssumeUnderTest which adds too many assumptions to the tests, especially for complex and large objects, you can instead encapsulated/containize your test parameters.
In ArrayList case, you can create your own class implementing IList and using that as your input to your method. This gives you more control over the input scenarios and also allows you to test private methods if required (you would use a wrapper class implementing the same interface that has the logic of your private method):
public class TestList : IList<int>
{
    ArrayList innerList = new ArrayList();

    public int this[int index] 
    { 
        get { return (int)innerList[index]; } 
        set { innerList[index] = value; } 
    }

    //... implement other IList<T> methods...
}

Then use it--------------------	    Assert.True(New_arrListContainsX);

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that last index of x in new array list is more or equal last index of x in old array list @@@ last index of x in a new Arraylist is geq or more than that in an old Arraylist of x
	    Assert.True(New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);"
	    Assert.True(New_Ret == Old_arrListCount);
        }
    }
